The program is used to display or update the or in the first sec‐
tor (sector 0) of a disk that uses the MBR style of partitioning.
The  following  ports use this style of disk partitioning: amd64,
arc,  bebox,  cobalt,  hpcarm,  hpcmips,  hpcsh,  i386,   macppc,
mvmeppc,  netwinder, ofppc, playstation2, and prep.  The MBR con‐
tains bootable code, a partition table, an  indication  of  which
partition  is and (optionally, depending on the boot code) a menu
for selecting a partition to be booted.  There can be at  most  4
partitions  defined  in sector 0, one of which can be an extended
partition which can be split into any  number  of  sub‐partitions
(then  called  logical  partitions).  The boot code in the MBR is
usually invoked by the BIOS or firmware, and the MBR passes  con‐
trol  to  the  next stage boot code stored in the first sector of
the partition to be booted (the or After booting,  does  not  use
the  partitioning  done  by  instead it uses a disklabel saved in
sector 1 of the partition.  See for a way  of  using  information
from  the  MBR  to  construct a disklabel.  The standard MBR boot
code will only boot the partition.  However, contains  additional
boot  programs which allow the user to interactively select which
of the partitions to boot.  The code will also boot from  an  ex‐
tended  partition  but  will  not work on old systems that do not
